 largely negative and supports the diagnosis of lcis  however  there is weak membrane staining in the ductal cells in the areas of cribriform architecture supporting a diagnosis dcis  stains for p63  myosin  calponin and keratin performed on slides  removed_accession_id    removed_accession_id   and  removed_accession_id  do not show evidence of an invasive carcinoma  overall  the morphologic findings are compatible with carcinoma in situ with both ductal and lobular features  immunostains for estrogen receptor and progesterone receptor will be reported in an addendum  this case was amended to correct the final diagnosis  on initial review  the findings were interpreted as extensive lcis  however  upon review of the e cadherin as well as the er  pr and her 2neu stain the findings were interpreted as both ductal and lobular carcinoma in situ  the final diagnosis and note in the report has been revised to reflect these changes  by his her signature below  the pathologist listed as making the final diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  gross description  received fresh   labeled remove_patient_name  donovan  remove_mrn and  left breast needle loc   is an unoriented portion of fibroadipose tissue  5 0 x removed_date x 0 6   with two needle localization wires in place  the specimen is entirely inked black and serially sectioned to reveal no mass lesions  the specimen is entirely included in  removed_accession_id  with   prior biopsy site in  removed_accession_id   addendum note  reason  1 quantitative results of immunohistochemical staining for receptor proteins breast  left  block  removed_accession_id    the ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein  the ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  the ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells show strong  3   overexpression of her 2 c erbb 2  see note  note  estrogen and proges